# Pinned sibling tool versions — the SINGLE source of truth for CI.
#
# Why SHAs and not `--depth 1` of main (what this used to do): a demo whose CI clones HEAD is green or red
# depending on what the siblings looked like that morning, and an old run can never be reproduced. It also
# breaks outright when a sibling's history is rewritten — which has happened here.
#
# Why not npm/semver: arbor resolves copse and coir from a PATH (config or env, deliberately), so npm's
# resolution never runs on that edge. Package versions are also unreliable during 0.x — the interfaces move
# without the numbers moving. The runtime guard for THAT is the contract version (arbor src/contract.mjs);
# these pins are the reproducibility half, and the two are independent on purpose: a pin says "the same
# code as last time", a contract version says "a shape I can actually read".
#
# To bump: move the SHA, run the gate, commit the change with the reason. Both workflows read this file via
#   - run: cat ci/pins.env >> $GITHUB_ENV
# so a pin lives in exactly one place.

COIR_SHA=37ac408b9976b00778c76945d26f179f83b990d8
COPSE_SHA=989e499491c02fbc71d570a2b8ec04fac1b18ee8
ARBOR_SHA=4ab625d669820b7c0c636271ee2b57fcdabf2a91
